WHAT IS DCL?
------------
DCL stands for: Data Control Language

DCL commands are used to control:
  • Access permissions
  • User privileges
  • Security in database

================================================================================
PURPOSE OF DCL
================================================================================

DCL helps:
  1. Grant permissions
  2. Remove permissions
  3. Secure database objects

================================================================================
MAIN DCL COMMANDS
================================================================================

There are mainly two DCL commands:
  1. GRANT
  2. REVOKE

================================================================================
1. GRANT COMMAND
================================================================================

Definition: GRANT gives permissions to users.

Syntax:
  GRANT privilege_name
  ON table_name
  TO user_name;

Example 1 – Grant SELECT Permission:
  GRANT SELECT
  ON Employees
  TO Ravi;

Meaning: User Ravi can read Employees table.

Example 2 – Grant Multiple Permissions:
  GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE
  ON Employees
  TO Ravi;

Meaning: Ravi can:
  • Read data
  • Insert data
  • Update data

GRANT Execution Flow (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Admin identifies user (Ravi).
  Step 2: Select permissions (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE).
  Step 3: Database stores permission.
  Step 4: User gets access.

Diagram:
  Admin
     ↓
  GRANT Permission
     ↓
  Database Updates Access Rights
     ↓
  User Gets Access

================================================================================
COMMON PRIVILEGES IN GRANT
================================================================================

+----------------+-------------------+
| Privilege      | Purpose           |
+----------------+-------------------+
| SELECT         | Read data         |
| INSERT         | Add rows          |
| UPDATE         | Modify rows       |
| DELETE         | Remove rows       |
| ALL PRIVILEGES | Full access       |
+----------------+-------------------+

Grant ALL PRIVILEGES Example:
  GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES
  ON Employees
  TO Ravi;

Meaning: Ravi gets complete access.

================================================================================
WITH GRANT OPTION
================================================================================

Definition: Allows the user to grant permissions to others.

Example:
  GRANT SELECT
  ON Employees
  TO Ravi
  WITH GRANT OPTION;

Meaning: Ravi can now give SELECT permission to other users.

Diagram:
  Admin
     ↓
  GRANT with GRANT OPTION
     ↓
  Ravi
     ↓
  Can Grant Permissions to Others

================================================================================
2. REVOKE COMMAND
================================================================================

Definition: REVOKE removes permissions from users.

Syntax:
  REVOKE privilege_name
  ON table_name
  FROM user_name;

Example:
  REVOKE INSERT
  ON Employees
  FROM Ravi;

Meaning: Ravi can no longer insert rows.

REVOKE Execution Flow (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Find user permissions.
  Step 2: Remove specified privilege.
  Step 3: Update database security.
  Step 4: User loses access.

Diagram:
  Admin
     ↓
  REVOKE Permission
     ↓
  Database Removes Access
     ↓
  User Access Restricted

Example – Remove All Permissions:
  REVOKE ALL PRIVILEGES
  ON Employees
  FROM Ravi;

================================================================================
DIFFERENCE BETWEEN GRANT AND REVOKE
================================================================================

+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| GRANT                    | REVOKE                   |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Gives permissions        | Removes permissions      |
| Allows access            | Restricts access         |
| Adds privileges          | Cancels privileges       |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
DCL EXECUTION ORDER (EXAMPLE)
================================================================================

Example:
  GRANT SELECT ON Employees TO Ravi;
  REVOKE UPDATE ON Employees FROM Ravi;

Execution Flow:
  Step 1: Grant SELECT permission.
  Step 2: User can read data.
  Step 3: Remove UPDATE permission.
  Step 4: User cannot modify data.

================================================================================
REAL-TIME EXAMPLE – COMPANY DATABASE
================================================================================

HR Team needs: SELECT, UPDATE
Interns need only: SELECT

DCL controls these permissions.

Database Security Diagram:
  Users
     ↓
  DCL Commands
     ↓
  Access Control
     ↓
  Secure Database

================================================================================
IMPORTANT POINTS
================================================================================

1. DCL manages permissions.
2. Mainly used by DBA (Database Administrator).
3. Provides database security.
4. Controls user access.

================================================================================
DIFFERENCES WITH OTHER SQL COMMANDS
================================================================================

DCL vs TCL:
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| DCL                      | TCL                      |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Controls permissions     | Controls transactions    |
| GRANT, REVOKE            | COMMIT, ROLLBACK         |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

DCL vs DDL:
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| DCL                      | DDL                      |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Security related         | Structure related        |
| GRANT, REVOKE            | CREATE, ALTER, DROP      |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES
================================================================================

Advantages:
  1. Improves security
  2. Controls user access
  3. Prevents unauthorized changes
  4. Supports multi-user systems

Disadvantages:
  1. Permission management can become complex
  2. Wrong privileges may create security risks

================================================================================
QUICK REVISION
================================================================================

+----------+-------------------+
| Command  | Purpose           |
+----------+-------------------+
| GRANT    | Give permission   |
| REVOKE   | Remove permission |
+----------+-------------------+

Memory Trick:
  GRANT  → Give Access
  REVOKE → Remove Access

================================================================================
SQL EXECUTION EXAMPLE
================================================================================

  GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON Employees TO Ravi;
  REVOKE INSERT ON Employees FROM Ravi;

Final Result:
+------------+---------+
| Permission | Status  |
+------------+---------+
| SELECT     | Allowed |
| INSERT     | Removed |
+------------+---------+

================================================================================
INTERVIEW POINTS
================================================================================

1. DCL stands for Data Control Language.
2. Used for database security.
3. GRANT gives privileges.
4. REVOKE removes privileges.
5. DBA mainly uses DCL commands.
6. WITH GRANT OPTION allows users to grant permissions further.
7. DCL controls database access rights.
